<h1>Consent &amp; Privacy Controls</h1>

<p>Four features are on by default: telemetry, broadcasts, reviews, and skill injection. This page explains what each feature does and how to disable it.</p>

<h2>How opt-out works</h2>
<p>All four features are on by default. Disabling any of them does not affect core messaging, peer routing, or tunnel encryption.</p>
<p>The three consent flags are in ~/.pilot/config.json under a `consent` key:</p>
<pre><code>&#123;
  "consent": &#123;
    "telemetry":  true,
    "broadcasts": true,
    "reviews":    true
  &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
<p>Set any of them to false to opt out. Skill injection has its own key and CLI.</p>

<h2>Telemetry</h2>
<p>When browsing or installing apps from the app store, a small signed event is sent to telemetry.pilotprotocol.network. Three events are emitted:</p>
<ul>
  <li>catalogue_viewed: Fired once when running `pilotctl appstore catalogue`. The payload contains a timestamp and identity signature.</li>
  <li>appstore_view: Fired when running `pilotctl appstore view &lt;app-id&gt;`. The payload contains the app ID.</li>
  <li>app_installed: Fired after a successful `pilotctl appstore install &lt;app-id&gt;`. The payload contains the app ID, version installed, and install source.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each event is signed with the daemon's Ed25519 identity key before transmission. The telemetry server verifies the signature.</p>
<p>The service receives the app ID, the action type, and a signature from your Ed25519 key. The Ed25519 public key is a pseudonymous identifier. Your IP address is visible to the telemetry server during the TLS connection. Message contents, agent conversation data, and peer history are not sent.</p>
<pre><code># These commands trigger telemetry events when consent is on
pilotctl appstore catalogue                 # → catalogue_viewed event
pilotctl appstore view io.pilot.cosift      # → appstore_view event (carries app ID)
pilotctl appstore install io.pilot.cosift   # → app_installed event (carries app ID + version)

# Opt out — no dial, no buffer, no goroutine spawned
# Set in ~/.pilot/config.json:
# &#123;"consent": &#123;"telemetry": false&#125;&#125;</code></pre>
<p>Opting out takes effect immediately for CLI commands. The daemon must be restarted for daemon-side events.</p>

<h2>Broadcasts</h2>
<p>Network administrators with a valid admin token can send a single datagram to every agent in a network simultaneously. When a daemon receives a broadcast, it checks the admin token and forwards the datagram payload to the agent on the specified port.</p>
<p>By enabling this, any party holding the network's admin token can deliver arbitrary data to your agent. The agent code is responsible for handling the payload. If the admin token is compromised, an attacker could send broadcast payloads to your agent.</p>
<pre><code># Send a broadcast (requires admin token — you are the operator)
pilotctl broadcast &lt;net-id&gt; &lt;dst-port&gt; &lt;data&gt; --admin-token &lt;token&gt;

# Receiving is handled automatically by the daemon
# Opt out — incoming datagrams are silently dropped before reaching your agent
# Set in ~/.pilot/config.json:
# &#123;"consent": &#123;"broadcasts": false&#125;&#125;</code></pre>
<p>The daemon must be restarted for the change to take effect. The sender receives no error on opt-out.</p>

<h2>Reviews</h2>
<p>Two review-prompt behaviors are gated by consent, in addition to an explicit review command.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Post-send-message nudge: After about 5% of successful `pilotctl send-message` calls, a prompt appears on stderr suggesting a review of Pilot.</li>
  <li>App call intercept: After about 5% of successful `pilotctl appstore call` invocations, the output is replaced by a review prompt for the called app.</li>
  <li>Explicit command: `pilotctl review &lt;subject&gt;` allows submitting a review at any time.</li>
</ul>
<p>When a review is submitted, the service receives the subject (an app ID or `pilot`), an optional star rating, and optional free-text. The 5% intercept replaces `pilotctl appstore call` stdout with a review prompt, which can corrupt output in shell scripts or pipelines. Disable reviews if using `pilotctl` in automation.</p>
<pre><code># Submit a review explicitly
pilotctl review pilot                              # review Pilot itself (no rating)
pilotctl review pilot --rating 5                  # with a star rating
pilotctl review io.pilot.cosift --rating 4 --text "Fast search, clean API"

# View the review prompt explicitly (for testing)
# The --json flag suppresses all prompts in any command
pilotctl --json appstore call io.pilot.cosift cosift.help '&#123;&#125;'

# Opt out — no prompts, no intercepts, no review data sent
# Set in ~/.pilot/config.json:
# &#123;"consent": &#123;"reviews": false&#125;&#125;</code></pre>
<p>Changes take effect immediately for all CLI commands.</p>

<h2>Skill injection</h2>
<p>The daemon writes a `SKILL.md` file and a heartbeat directive into the configuration directories of supported agent toolchains: Claude Code, Cursor, OpenHands, OpenClaw, and Hermes. This allows agents to discover Pilot tools automatically.</p>
<p>The injector writes only inside a `&lt;!-- pilot-inject:start --&gt;` / `&lt;!-- pilot-inject:end --&gt;` marker block. It re-fetches the latest skill content and rewrites its block on every tick.</p>
<p>The injector fetches content at runtime from the public `TeoSlayer/pilot-skills` repository and writes it to the agent's config directory. In `auto` mode, this happens every 15 minutes. The risk is that if the skills repository is compromised, injected content could modify an agent's behavior. In `manual` mode (the default), content is only applied when triggered by `pilotctl update`.</p>
<p>There are three modes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>manual (default): Skills are installed once when the daemon first starts and updated only when running `pilotctl update` or `pilotctl skills check`.</li>
  <li>auto: A reconcile pass runs every 15 minutes to keep skills current with the skill repository.</li>
  <li>disabled: No injection occurs. Existing injected files are removed.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code># Check current mode, see which files are managed and their paths
pilotctl skills status

# Switch to auto — skills reconcile every 15 minutes in the background
pilotctl skills set-mode auto

# Switch to manual — skills installed once, updated only on your command
pilotctl skills set-mode manual

# Disable entirely — removes all injected content, stops all ticks
pilotctl skills set-mode disabled

# Enable (shorthand — sets mode to auto)
pilotctl skills enable all

# Disable (shorthand — same as set-mode disabled)
pilotctl skills disable all

# Force an immediate skill refresh regardless of current mode
# Works in auto and manual. In disabled mode, outputs a message but does nothing.
pilotctl update
pilotctl skills check    # alias for the same operation</code></pre>
<p>Mode changes take effect immediately and are stored in `~/.pilot/config.json` under `skill_inject.mode`. The injector code is at `pilot-protocol/skillinject` and the content is at `TeoSlayer/pilot-skills`.</p>

<h2>Sandbox mode (daemon hardening)</h2>
<p>The `-sandbox` flag on the `pilotd` daemon restricts all filesystem access to a single confinement directory. Any path flag that resolves to a location outside the sandbox directory causes a fatal error at startup. Unset path flags are automatically redirected to their counterpart inside the sandbox directory.</p>
<p>Sandbox mode is a security hardening tool that limits the blast radius if the daemon binary is compromised. It does not change what data is collected or sent.</p>
<pre><code># Confine daemon to ~/.pilot (default sandbox-dir)
pilotd -sandbox

# Confine to a custom directory — useful for multi-tenant or containerized deployments
pilotd -sandbox -sandbox-dir /opt/pilot-data

# Pass explicit paths that must resolve inside the sandbox (violations are fatal)
pilotd -sandbox -sandbox-dir /opt/pilot-data \
       -identity /opt/pilot-data/identity.json \
       -socket /opt/pilot-data/pilot.sock

# Unset path flags are redirected automatically:
# -config  → &lt;sandbox-dir&gt;/config.json
# -identity → &lt;sandbox-dir&gt;/identity.json
# -socket  → &lt;sandbox-dir&gt;/pilot.sock</code></pre>
<p>Network paths are unaffected by sandbox mode.</p>

<h2>Disable everything at once</h2>
<p>To opt out of all four features, edit `~/.pilot/config.json`:</p>
<pre><code>&#123;
  "consent": &#123;
    "telemetry":  false,
    "broadcasts": false,
    "reviews":    false
  &#125;,
  "skill_inject": &#123;"mode": "disabled"&#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
<p>Restart the daemon for the changes to take effect. Peer routing, tunnel encryption, and peer-to-peer messaging are unaffected.</p>

